{"name":"Petrol Green","hex":"#549b8c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"petrol","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fluid consisting of a mixture of refined petroleum hydrocarbons, primarily consisting of octane, commonly used as a motor fuel.","A motor vehicle powered by petrol (as opposed to diesel)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fill or supply (a vehicle, etc.) with petrol."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/petrol"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gasoline","blocks":[{"text":"Gasoline or petrol is a petrochemical product, a transparent, yellowish and flammable liquid normally used as a fuel for spark-ignited internal combustion engines. It is chemically composed of organic compounds derived from the fractional distillation of petroleum and later chemically enhanced with gasoline additives. It is a high-volume profitable product produced in crude oil refineries."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Gasoline_in_mason_jar.jpg/330px-Gasoline_in_mason_jar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gasoline","wikidata":"Q39558"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/GasolineComp.png/500px-GasolineComp.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/GasolineComp.png/1280px-GasolineComp.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/GasolineComp.png/500px-GasolineComp.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Some of the components of gasoline: isooctane, butane, 3-ethyltoluene, and the octane enhancer MTBE","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGasolineComp.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Nodding_donkey.jpg/500px-Nodding_donkey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Nodding_donkey.jpg/500px-Nodding_donkey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A pumpjack in the United States","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANodding_donkey.jpg"},{"srcset":"